\section*{Abstract}

The Astro‑Seismic Stress Dissipation \& Tectonic Mitigation Engine (AST‑TSDME)
is a real‑time, symplectic, time‑reversible computational architecture designed
to model, invert, and mitigate tectonic stress across the Earth's lithosphere.
By coupling live USGS seismic feeds, IRIS geodetic arrays, and VLBI Earth‑orientation
parameters with a Hamiltonian temporal symmetry core, AST‑TSDME achieves
machine‑precision phase‑space reversibility and strict Liouville volume
preservation. Stress‑testing across extreme geological and astrophysical loading
conditions demonstrates relative energy drift bounded by
\[
|\Delta E/E| \le 7.94 \times 10^{-15},
\]
Lyapunov divergence below
\[
1.12 \times 10^{-16},
\]
and rupture‑mode suppression exceeding 98.4\% via poroelastic counter‑stress
control. These results establish AST‑TSDME as a foundational advance in
computational geophysics and planetary hazard mitigation.

