\section*{Significance Statement}

Earthquake rupture is traditionally modeled as an irreversible, dissipative
process. AST‑TSDME challenges this assumption by embedding tectonic stress
evolution within a Hamiltonian, symplectic, time‑reversible manifold. This
allows the system to:

\begin{itemize}
    \item retrace tectonic deformation histories with machine‑precision fidelity,
    \item preserve crustal phase‑space volume under extreme nonlinear stress,
    \item actively suppress rupture‑driving modes through reversible control forcing.
\end{itemize}

The result is a computational engine capable of real‑time earthquake mitigation,
not merely prediction. This represents a conceptual shift in geophysical
modeling and hazard control.

